1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,440
Two bottles, both labelled zero point one zero

2
00:00:03,560 --> 00:00:05,080
zero moles per litre.

3
00:00:05,360 --> 00:00:08,480
One's hydrochloric acid, the other's ethanoic acid.

4
00:00:08,960 --> 00:00:10,480
You've only got a pH meter.

5
00:00:10,880 --> 00:00:12,880
Can you show which acid is strong?

6
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:15,600
And here's the harder part: what if one

7
00:00:15,680 --> 00:00:17,040
label is wrong?

8
00:00:17,280 --> 00:00:20,160
Today you'll build two pH tests of strength,

9
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:22,320
and the second one doesn't trust the label

10
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:23,200
at all.

11
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:26,240
The first test is from the strong and

12
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:28,720
weak video, and the last practical used it

13
00:00:28,800 --> 00:00:31,760
too: compare at the same concentration.

14
00:00:32,240 --> 00:00:33,200
Why does that matter?

15
00:00:33,600 --> 00:00:35,920
Because a concentrated weak acid can read a

16
00:00:36,080 --> 00:00:38,600
lower pH than a dilute strong one.

17
00:00:38,880 --> 00:00:41,840
So if the concentrations differ, the pH mixes

18
00:00:41,920 --> 00:00:43,040
up two things.

19
00:00:43,440 --> 00:00:46,040
Match them, and any pH difference has to

20
00:00:46,080 --> 00:00:47,320
come from strength.

21
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:50,080
This time, all four are at zero point

22
00:00:50,160 --> 00:00:51,440
one zero zero.

23
00:00:52,667 --> 00:00:55,626
Measure them exactly as in the last practical:

24
00:00:55,707 --> 00:00:58,506
calibrate with buffers, rinse and blot the probe

25
00:00:58,587 --> 00:01:01,706
between samples, wait for a steady reading, and

26
00:01:01,787 --> 00:01:04,506
take three readings in fresh portions.

27
00:01:04,907 --> 00:01:07,626
One new point: the strong acid and base

28
00:01:07,707 --> 00:01:10,586
will read near one and thirteen, outside the

29
00:01:10,667 --> 00:01:12,586
four to ten range you calibrated.

30
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:15,680
Here are one class's means.

31
00:01:15,960 --> 00:01:19,160
Hydrochloric acid, one point zero eight.

32
00:01:19,240 --> 00:01:22,200
Ethanoic acid, two point nine zero.

33
00:01:22,240 --> 00:01:26,520
Sodium hydroxide, twelve point nine one, and ammonia,

34
00:01:26,640 --> 00:01:28,320
eleven point one zero.

35
00:01:28,600 --> 00:01:30,920
Each set of three readings agreed within about

36
00:01:31,080 --> 00:01:32,760
zero point zero three.

37
00:01:33,967 --> 00:01:37,646
At the same concentration, hydrochloric acid reads almost

38
00:01:37,727 --> 00:01:39,166
two units lower.

39
00:01:39,487 --> 00:01:42,046
Two units would be a hundred times, and

40
00:01:42,087 --> 00:01:43,966
this is a bit less, so from these

41
00:01:44,047 --> 00:01:47,366
readings it's about seventy times as much hydronium.

42
00:01:47,647 --> 00:01:50,766
That's because every hydrochloric acid molecule gives its

43
00:01:50,847 --> 00:01:53,806
proton to water, but only a small fraction

44
00:01:53,887 --> 00:01:55,806
of ethanoic acid molecules do.

45
00:01:56,287 --> 00:01:58,046
Single arrow, double arrow.

46
00:01:58,527 --> 00:02:01,966
The bases mirror it: sodium hydroxide gives far

47
00:02:02,047 --> 00:02:04,206
more hydroxide than ammonia does.

48
00:02:05,434 --> 00:02:08,233
But test one trusts the labels, and a

49
00:02:08,314 --> 00:02:09,913
wrong label could fool you.

50
00:02:10,154 --> 00:02:12,033
So here's the second test.

51
00:02:12,314 --> 00:02:15,433
Use a volumetric pipette to transfer ten millilitres

52
00:02:15,554 --> 00:02:18,473
of each solution into a hundred millilitre volumetric

53
00:02:18,634 --> 00:02:20,633
flask, and make it up to the mark

54
00:02:20,714 --> 00:02:22,233
with distilled water.

55
00:02:22,314 --> 00:02:26,153
That's exactly tenfold dilution, whatever the starting concentration

56
00:02:26,234 --> 00:02:27,113
was.

57
00:02:27,194 --> 00:02:29,513
Then see how far the pH moves.

58
00:02:30,734 --> 00:02:31,693
Here's what happened.

59
00:02:32,174 --> 00:02:34,973
Hydrochloric acid went from one point zero eight

60
00:02:35,214 --> 00:02:38,573
to two point zero four, up almost exactly

61
00:02:38,734 --> 00:02:39,613
one unit.

62
00:02:39,854 --> 00:02:42,493
Ethanoic acid went from two point nine zero

63
00:02:42,734 --> 00:02:45,533
to three point three nine, up only about

64
00:02:45,694 --> 00:02:46,493
half a unit.

65
00:02:46,894 --> 00:02:47,693
And the bases?

66
00:02:48,094 --> 00:02:51,773
Sodium hydroxide dropped about one unit, ammonia about

67
00:02:51,854 --> 00:02:52,493
half.

68
00:02:53,700 --> 00:02:55,380
Why about a whole unit?

69
00:02:55,620 --> 00:02:58,900
Hydrochloric acid is already completely ionised.

70
00:02:59,380 --> 00:03:02,260
Diluting it just spreads the same hydronium ions

71
00:03:02,340 --> 00:03:04,900
through ten times the volume, so there's a

72
00:03:04,980 --> 00:03:07,380
tenth as much hydronium in each litre.

73
00:03:07,860 --> 00:03:10,180
A tenth is one pH unit.

74
00:03:11,400 --> 00:03:13,480
Ethanoic acid is different.

75
00:03:13,720 --> 00:03:16,520
When you dilute it, the hydronium and ethanoate

76
00:03:16,600 --> 00:03:18,920
ions are spread out, so they meet less

77
00:03:19,120 --> 00:03:22,360
often, and fewer join back into whole molecules.

78
00:03:22,440 --> 00:03:25,640
The balance settles with a bigger fraction ionised.

79
00:03:25,760 --> 00:03:27,960
Those extra ions partly make up for the

80
00:03:28,040 --> 00:03:29,160
dilution.

81
00:03:29,200 --> 00:03:31,640
So the hydronium per litre falls by much

82
00:03:31,760 --> 00:03:34,440
less than ten times, and for ethanoic acid

83
00:03:34,680 --> 00:03:37,320
the pH rises only about half a unit.

84
00:03:38,534 --> 00:03:40,133
The bases mirror this.

85
00:03:40,374 --> 00:03:43,893
Ammonia only partly reacts with water, so dilution

86
00:03:44,014 --> 00:03:46,373
lets more of it react, and its pH

87
00:03:46,454 --> 00:03:48,573
falls only about half a unit.

88
00:03:48,854 --> 00:03:51,493
The diluted readings match the last practical, too.

89
00:03:52,214 --> 00:03:53,733
But look at the gap between the two

90
00:03:53,854 --> 00:03:57,733
acids: almost two units before dilution, about one

91
00:03:57,814 --> 00:03:59,173
point four after.

92
00:03:59,814 --> 00:04:02,133
That's why the last practical found about twenty

93
00:04:02,294 --> 00:04:04,133
three times, not seventy.

94
00:04:04,694 --> 00:04:07,413
Dilution narrows the gap, because the weak acid

95
00:04:07,574 --> 00:04:08,773
ionises more.

96
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:11,360
So, the variables.

97
00:04:11,440 --> 00:04:14,800
In test one, the independent variable is which

98
00:04:14,880 --> 00:04:17,120
acid or base you use, and in test

99
00:04:17,200 --> 00:04:18,720
two it's the dilution.

100
00:04:19,280 --> 00:04:21,920
The dependent variable is the pH, or its

101
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:22,560
change.

102
00:04:23,040 --> 00:04:26,160
And you control the concentration, the temperature, the

103
00:04:26,240 --> 00:04:28,960
volume in each beaker, the calibrated meter and

104
00:04:29,040 --> 00:04:30,160
the rinsing routine.

105
00:04:31,367 --> 00:04:34,366
Validity: does the test measure strength?

106
00:04:34,647 --> 00:04:37,846
Matching the concentration makes test one valid, and

107
00:04:37,927 --> 00:04:40,966
test two checks it without trusting the labels.

108
00:04:41,287 --> 00:04:45,286
Reliability: repeats agree within a few hundredths, and

109
00:04:45,367 --> 00:04:46,846
across two sessions.

110
00:04:47,127 --> 00:04:50,486
Accuracy: the readings near one and thirteen are

111
00:04:50,607 --> 00:04:53,206
less certain, outside the calibration.

112
00:04:53,247 --> 00:04:56,006
But the conclusions rest on big gaps, so

113
00:04:56,087 --> 00:04:57,046
they still hold.

114
00:04:58,267 --> 00:04:59,706
The risk assessment.

115
00:05:00,027 --> 00:05:02,906
Zero point one zero zero moles per litre

116
00:05:02,987 --> 00:05:06,746
sodium hydroxide sits just under the irritant limit,

117
00:05:06,867 --> 00:05:09,066
so treat a splash as harmful and wear

118
00:05:09,147 --> 00:05:10,106
safety glasses.

119
00:05:10,667 --> 00:05:12,906
The acids and ammonia are well below their

120
00:05:12,987 --> 00:05:15,146
limits, but glasses stay on.

121
00:05:15,627 --> 00:05:18,426
The concentrated stocks are corrosive and stay with

122
00:05:18,507 --> 00:05:19,066
your teacher.

123
00:05:19,467 --> 00:05:21,786
And handle the pipette with a filler, never

124
00:05:21,867 --> 00:05:22,586
by mouth.

125
00:05:23,800 --> 00:05:27,400
Here's the rule to remember: match, then compare.

126
00:05:27,880 --> 00:05:30,840
And when you dilute tenfold, strong moves about

127
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:33,560
one, weak moves about half.

128
00:05:34,767 --> 00:05:35,686
Your turn.

129
00:05:35,967 --> 00:05:39,006
A student measures zero point five zero moles

130
00:05:39,087 --> 00:05:41,966
per litre methanoic acid at pH two point

131
00:05:42,047 --> 00:05:45,566
zero three, and zero point zero zero two

132
00:05:45,687 --> 00:05:48,286
zero moles per litre nitric acid at two

133
00:05:48,447 --> 00:05:50,046
point seven zero.

134
00:05:50,127 --> 00:05:53,086
She concludes methanoic acid is stronger.

135
00:05:53,407 --> 00:05:56,446
Evaluate her conclusion, and describe how she should

136
00:05:56,607 --> 00:05:57,886
change her method.

137
00:05:58,047 --> 00:05:59,326
Pause here to answer.

138
00:06:09,734 --> 00:06:11,773
Her conclusion isn't valid.

139
00:06:11,974 --> 00:06:14,613
The concentrations differ by two hundred and fifty

140
00:06:14,694 --> 00:06:18,133
times, so the pH depends on concentration as

141
00:06:18,214 --> 00:06:19,293
well as strength.

142
00:06:19,574 --> 00:06:21,573
She should test both acids at the same

143
00:06:21,654 --> 00:06:24,613
concentration, with the same meter and temperature.

144
00:06:25,174 --> 00:06:28,773
Then nitric acid, which ionises completely, would read

145
00:06:28,854 --> 00:06:29,253
lower.

146
00:06:29,894 --> 00:06:33,333
"Lower pH, so stronger acid" is the trap.

147
00:06:34,534 --> 00:06:35,373
One more.

148
00:06:35,654 --> 00:06:38,613
An acid of unknown concentration has a pH

149
00:06:38,694 --> 00:06:40,853
of two point four zero.

150
00:06:40,934 --> 00:06:43,893
She dilutes it exactly tenfold with a volumetric

151
00:06:43,974 --> 00:06:46,773
pipette and flask, and the pH becomes two

152
00:06:46,934 --> 00:06:48,533
point nine zero.

153
00:06:48,614 --> 00:06:50,693
Is the acid strong or weak?

154
00:06:50,934 --> 00:06:52,373
Justify your answer.

155
00:06:52,694 --> 00:06:54,053
Pause here to answer.

156
00:07:04,467 --> 00:07:05,546
It's weak.

157
00:07:05,827 --> 00:07:08,466
Ten millilitres to a hundred is a tenfold

158
00:07:08,507 --> 00:07:09,186
dilution.

159
00:07:09,667 --> 00:07:11,426
A strong acid would leave a tenth of

160
00:07:11,507 --> 00:07:14,146
the hydronium and rise about one unit, to

161
00:07:14,307 --> 00:07:15,746
three point four.

162
00:07:15,827 --> 00:07:18,626
This one rose only zero point five zero,

163
00:07:18,747 --> 00:07:21,746
so more molecules ionised as it was diluted,

164
00:07:21,827 --> 00:07:23,746
and that only happens when an acid is

165
00:07:23,907 --> 00:07:25,426
partly ionised.

166
00:07:25,507 --> 00:07:27,906
A low starting pH tells you nothing about

167
00:07:27,987 --> 00:07:29,106
strength on its own.

168
00:07:30,334 --> 00:07:32,893
So, to use pH to show strength:

169
00:07:33,454 --> 00:07:37,613
match the concentrations and compare, or dilute exactly

170
00:07:37,694 --> 00:07:40,413
tenfold and see how far the pH moves.

171
00:07:40,894 --> 00:07:44,413
Strong moves about one unit, weak moves less.

172
00:07:44,734 --> 00:07:47,053
But why does chloride never take its proton

173
00:07:47,134 --> 00:07:48,893
back, while ethanoate does?

174
00:07:50,174 --> 00:07:52,013
Next: conjugate pairs, and the

175
00:07:52,574 --> 00:07:54,253
species that can go both ways.

176
00:07:55,467 --> 00:07:56,906
Here are the key notes.

177
00:07:57,147 --> 00:07:59,066
Pause the video if you need more time

178
00:07:59,147 --> 00:07:59,946
to copy them.
